---
import '~/assets/styles/tailwind.css';
import CommonMeta from '~/components/common/CommonMeta.astro';
import Metadata from '~/components/common/Metadata.astro';
import Favicons from '~/components/Favicons.astro';
import { getCanonical } from '~/utils/permalinks';
import AsciiRuntime from '~/components/joyan/AsciiRuntime.astro';
import AsciiScene from '~/components/joyan/AsciiScene.astro';
import SceneCanvas from '~/components/joyan/SceneCanvas.astro';
import DitherScene from '~/components/joyan/DitherScene.astro';
const metadata = {
  title: 'ASCII comparison lab',
  description: 'Isolated comparison of baked dither and experimental runtime ASCII.',
  canonical: String(getCanonical('/lab/ascii')),
  robots: { index: false, follow: false },
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" dir="ltr">
  <head>
    <CommonMeta />
    <Favicons />
    <Metadata {...metadata} lang="en" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#0B0B0C" />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-ink-950 font-sans text-bone-100 antialiased">
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <a data-lab-return data-astro-reload href="/" class="font-mono text-sm text-bone-100 underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">← Return to landing</a>
    <a href="/lab/shapes" class="ml-4 font-mono text-sm text-bone-100 underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">Shape laboratory →</a>
    <a href="/lab/image" class="ml-4 font-mono text-sm text-bone-100 underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">Image laboratory →</a>
    <header class="my-10">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-plomo-400">Lab / WU13 / experimental only</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl tracking-tight text-bone-50 sm:text-5xl">Baked scenes. Live dots.</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-bone-100">Two delivery methods, not a production redesign. The existing J1 loop stays baked; the samples below run the real ascii.rest 0.4.0 library locally: two comparison pieces and a live gallery of the coloured dot scenes.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">Runtime art by bas3line · MIT · <a href="https://github.com/bas3line/ascii" class="underline underline-offset-4">source</a>. Vendor artwork and colors are demo-only, not Joyan brand tokens. No external catalogue or font downloads.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="border-t border-hairline py-8" aria-labelledby="baked-title">
      <h2 id="baked-title" class="text-xl text-bone-50">01 / Baked J1 · native video</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-plomo-400">Controlled composition, brand palette and exact poster. Native autoplay may run without JavaScript; reduced motion reveals the poster. The runtime controls below do not control this video.</p>
      <DitherScene />
    </section>
    <AsciiRuntime piece="donut" title="02 / Live donut · text" description="40 × 22 glyphs in a PRE. Shading comes from characters, using the existing local monospace font." />
    <AsciiRuntime piece="java" title="03 / Live cup · colored canvas" description="42 × 28 cells, palette-indexed red and blue glyphs. Vendor Java mark, experimental only—not a product endorsement or new branding. The package draws its own system-monospace atlas." />
    <section class="border-t border-hairline py-8" aria-labelledby="scenes-title">
      <h2 id="scenes-title" class="text-xl text-bone-50">04 / Live scenes · coloured dots</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">Six of the library's fifteen <span class="font-mono">scenes</span> pieces, mounted live from focused local imports at 12 fps. Each frame is 200 × 100 square cells drawn as dots only (<span class="font-mono">·</span>, <span class="font-mono">•</span>, <span class="font-mono">●</span>) over a fixed palette with ordered Bayer dithering and palette compensation — no ASCII glyph art and no remote asset. They are decorative: the caption beside each canvas names it and says what it shows, and each canvas is hidden from assistive technology.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">The library's other generative family, <span class="font-mono">looks</span>, draws character art in a different ramp and is not dithered by default. Its cost depends on how it is measured: bundling the whole recipes barrel minified (vite/esbuild, gzip level 6) is 324,343 B raw / 98,814 B gzip, and even tree-shaken to a single <span class="font-mono">sea()</span> it is 143,960 B raw / 44,252 B gzip. The package exports no <span class="font-mono">ascii.rest/kit/recipes/looks</span> leaf, and one real <span class="font-mono">sea()</span> example measured +44,134 B gzip on top of this gallery (limit: scene-only + 12,288 B). The cost did not fit, so this trial ships no <span class="font-mono">looks</span> example.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <AsciiScene scene="night-coast" index="scene 01" />
        <AsciiScene scene="misty-forest" index="scene 02" />
        <AsciiScene scene="desert-night" index="scene 03" />
        <AsciiScene scene="aurora-fjord" index="scene 04" />
        <AsciiScene scene="alpine-dawn" index="scene 05" />
        <AsciiScene scene="ocean-sunset" index="scene 06" />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="border-t border-hairline py-8" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
      <h2 id="compare-title" class="text-xl text-bone-50">05 / Our engine beside the library · one 200 × 100 grid</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
        Three panes, the same 200 × 100 grid and the same displayed size: the library's <span class="font-mono">night-coast</span> live,
        our own coastal night live, and our own coastal night baked. Ours is drawn by an engine we wrote
        (<span class="font-mono">scripts/scene-src/engine.mjs</span>): square cells, the dot ramp <span class="font-mono">· • ● ⬤</span>
        only, ordered 4×4 Bayer dithering, one indexed palette per scene, deterministic hash noise and per-cell palette
        compensation, so a small dot is carried by a brighter ink instead of disappearing into the dark ground. The same
        frame function draws the canvas and the PNG: the baked still is the engine's poster frame, not a lookalike.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
        <span class="text-bone-100">This is not a video.</span> Our live pane is a canvas the page paints frame by frame from the
        engine's frame function, and the baked pane is that same function's poster frame written to a PNG by Node — no codec,
        no movie, no external asset. Only section 01 is a video. A dot is a <span class="text-bone-100">round,
        anti-aliased disc</span>: each pixel receives the exact area of the disc inside it, and each step's radius is derived
        from the measured coverage ladder (0.1841 / 0.3804 / 0.6381 of a cell becomes a radius of 0.2421 / 0.3480 / 0.4507 of a
        cell), so every dot keeps a round rim and never a block. Since WU19 the ladder has a fifth rung — and since WU22
        that rung is the INSCRIBED DISC: coverage π/4 ≈ 0.7854, a radius of exactly 0.5 of a cell. WU19's original full-coverage
        rung (radius 0.7071, the circumscribed circle) covered the whole cell, so a saturated highlight read as a square —
        the user saw exactly that — and the ladder was capped, which honestly means highlights now top out at the largest
        round dot instead of closing the cell. The two live panes are <em>not</em> drawn at the same
        pixel pitch: our canvas is a fixed 1600 × 800 backing store (8 px per cell) that the browser resamples to the pane,
        while the library sizes its own canvas to its display box — measured 307 × 154, about 1.54 device px per cell, at a
        1440-wide viewport. Same grid, same displayed size, different internal resolution; and our image is no longer
        resampled with nearest-neighbour pixelation, which is what turned the old dots into squares.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
        Measured cost of each pane, shipped bytes at gzip level 6. The library's own bundle carries all eight library mounts
        on this page (two pieces, the six gallery scenes and this pane): <span class="font-mono">25256 B</span> gzip in one
        chunk, so that is not a per-pane number. Our engine, our scene, the WU17 shape catalogue, the WU18 image
        pipeline and overlays come to <span class="font-mono">21452 B</span> raw (<span class="font-mono">8838 B</span> gzip)
        and their runtime to <span class="font-mono">17363 B</span> raw (<span class="font-mono">6358 B</span> gzip), shipped as
        two chunks shared by the three laboratory routes (<span class="font-mono">/lab/ascii</span>,
        <span class="font-mono">/lab/shapes</span> and <span class="font-mono">/lab/image</span>) and, since WU20,
        the public coming-hero, measured at
        <span class="font-mono">8838 B</span> + <span class="font-mono">6358 B</span> gzip (WU27: the shared scene graph split
        into a runtime chunk and an engine chunk when the /lab/sequence route joined, and the warp overlay runs slower
        and wider); WU17 replaced the single-page inline module with those shared chunks.
        The baked still is <span class="font-mono">35270 B</span> of PNG at 1600 × 800 and ships no script at all.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <article data-ascii-compare="night-coast" class="flex min-w-0 flex-col border border-hairline">
          <div data-compare-frame class="compare-frame bg-ink-900">
            <canvas id="compare-night-coast" data-ascii-output aria-hidden="true" width="400" height="200"></canvas>
            <p data-ascii-fallback class="p-6 text-sm text-plomo-400">
              Static fallback: the library's night coast — a lighthouse turning its beam under moonlit clouds over a dark sea,
              200 × 100 dot cells. The live frame needs JavaScript.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4">
            <p data-ascii-caption class="min-w-0 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
              <span class="block font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-bone-100">01 · library night-coast · live</span>
              <span class="mt-1 block">The library's own scene, drawn by the library's own code, its own palette, its own art. Vendor artwork shown for comparison only, never as ours.</span>
              <span class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs">200 × 100 cells · dots (· • ●) · live canvas sized to this box (measured 307 × 154 at a 1440-wide viewport)</span>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-4 pb-3">
            <button type="button" disabled aria-controls="compare-night-coast" aria-pressed="false" class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-accent disabled:opacity-60">Play night-coast</button>
          </div>
          <p data-ascii-status class="border-t border-hairline px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-plomo-400" role="status">
            Static fallback · JavaScript unavailable
          </p>
        </article>
        <SceneCanvas scene="joyan-night-coast" variant="live" index="02 · ours · live" cost="engine + scene + catalogue + image pipeline + overlays: 8838 B gzip, plus the runtime at 6358 B gzip, in the /lab/** chunks shared with both other laboratories, the /lab/sequence route and, since WU20, the public coming-hero (WU27 re-measured); 1600 × 800 backing store (8 px per cell)" />
        <SceneCanvas scene="joyan-night-coast" variant="baked" index="03 · ours · baked" cost="the engine's own poster frame, 35270 B PNG, 1600 × 800, no JavaScript" />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
        Provenance, in three parts, and the licence boundary is now an explicit adoption ledger
        (<span class="font-mono">scripts/scene-src/adoption-ledger.json</span>). <span class="text-bone-100">Published standard
        material, used as published:</span> the canonical 4 × 4 ordered-dither matrix (Bayer, 1973) and the Rec.709 luma
        coefficients (ITU-R BT.709), which govern both the tone model and the chroma term.
        <span class="text-bone-100">Adopted from <span class="font-mono">ascii.rest</span> 0.4.0 (MIT, bas3line), with its notice
        retained in <span class="font-mono">scripts/scene-src/ascii.rest-LICENSE</span>:</span> the cell-sprite atlas — one
        finished cell per ramp step and palette ink, built once and copied into each cell, adapted from the glyph atlas in
        <span class="font-mono">ascii.rest/dist/mount.js</span>. What we improved beyond the original: the key is our ramp step
        and palette ink instead of a glyph and a colour, the set is bounded by the palette so nothing is ever evicted or
        re-rendered, and the same atlas feeds the Node bake and the live canvas, so an optimised blit cannot make them
        disagree (measured 9.5 ms per 200 × 100 frame at 8 px per cell against 12.2 ms for a per-pixel blend, byte-identical
        output). <span class="text-bone-100">Ideas learned from the library and reimplemented:</span> a dot ramp as a tone
        ladder with an ordered dither, per-cell colour compensation for how much ink a dot lays down, deterministic integer
        hash noise, and a per-scene indexed palette with a declared ground. <span class="text-bone-100">Ours:</span> the
        mixing function (our own constants and shift schedule), the two-axis wrapping lattice noise, the analytic anti-aliased
        dot masks and the radii derived from the measured ladder, the palette selection, the scene, the palette and every
        check. What we did not take: the library's font glyphs as our dots (ours do not depend on a font being installed), its
        scene catalogue, its artwork, or its colours as ours. Method and the full list:
        <span class="font-mono">docs/MOTOR-ESCENAS.md</span>.
      </p>
    </section>
    <footer class="border-t border-hairline pt-8 text-sm text-plomo-400">
      <p>12 fps maximum · pauses offscreen and in hidden tabs · static under reduced motion. Play restarts the library timeline. Without JavaScript, descriptions replace runtime art; the native baked video may still animate.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Scenes draw dots, keep square(1:1) cells, and are replayed at a 12 fps ceiling: the played rate can be lower under load. Their palettes are vendor demo art, not Joyan tokens, and their still frame under reduced motion is the package's own.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Our own engine's scene keeps the same 12 fps ceiling, pauses offscreen and in hidden tabs, and shows its
        poster frame under reduced motion. The baked still is the engine's own raster of that same poster frame, written by Node
        with no browser involved. Round dots with radii derived from the measured coverage ladder; no video, no codec and no
        external asset. Adapted <span class="font-mono">ascii.rest</span> code (MIT, bas3line) is listed in
        <span class="font-mono">scripts/scene-src/adoption-ledger.json</span> and its notice is retained in the repository.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Placement candidates: controlled stories and social assets stay baked; runtime may suit purposeful generative accents beside—not behind—content. Rollout remains pending human testing.</p>
    </footer>
  </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .compare-frame {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .compare-frame canvas {
    display: none;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }
  [data-ascii-compare][data-ready] canvas {
    display: block;
  }
  [data-ascii-compare][data-ready] [data-ascii-fallback] {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
